── ROUND 67: a warning that named the wrong reason, and bookkeeping that cannot be written ──
The `_attachJobController` catch had never run, and driving it turned up a SENTENCE rather than a branch: a
registry whose `attachController` throws was reported to the model as "no `jobs` service is composed in this
process" — a true statement about a composition and a false one about this machine, with different next
steps for whoever reads it. The reason is now carried from the place that knows it (`_jobControllerError`),
and the no-`jobs` sentence is unchanged character for character, because a round-55 assertion pins it
verbatim and must keep passing. That is the round's only product edit (7 code lines) — `host/lib/index.js`
fell 67 → 40 because the rest of the round's fixtures execute the bookkeeping failures around a render: a
progress tick whose write fails (reported ONCE, render unaffected), the failure record itself failing to be
written (renderer stopped, failure said out loud on the job's output, live job settled, record left at the
status it had), a nonzero exit with no error document at all (`NONZERO_EXIT` classified from the exit code),
a controller that refuses to attach, and a projection that refuses to be killed during a cancel.
`provider-local/lib/index.js` (38) and the mid-sized files were re-measured and unchanged.

One surviving mutation earned its place in that count: `if (progressFailures === 1)` → `true` stayed green
through TWO attempts, and both times the assertion was fine while the MEASUREMENT was wrong — one tick
fails twice (the pid write is swallowed by its own "mid-write" catch, so a naive write counter reaches two
after a single progress failure), the count was sampled before the second report could be appended, and the
Host's own startup reconciliation was writing a record for the live render on top of it. The fixture now
turns `reconcileOnStart` off, counts `_absorbProgress` call sites only, and takes the line count after the
render is over; the mutation is then red with two lines.

── ROUND 68: the last rung of the cancel ladder, and a bound nobody could lower ──
`cancelJob`'s escalation (a pid that is still there after `processGroup` has already tried SIGTERM, a grace
and SIGKILL) had never run, and the reason was not that nobody tried: nothing killable can reach it. What
can is a ZOMBIE — `kill(pid, 0)` answers yes and no signal changes anything — so the fixture builds one
with Node alone (a keeper that spawns a child and then blocks its thread on `Atomics.wait` forever never
reaps it, which is the entire trick) and WAITS for `ps -o stat=` to say `Z` before asking for the cancel.
Readings: both rungs attempted, `gone: false`, `escalated` present, `after.command` `<defunct>` — the
product says "escalated once, then reported", not "confirmed gone"; killing the keeper then makes the same
pid answer `gone`, which is what proves the earlier "alive" was a zombie rather than a renderer.

The real illness was a constant: two rungs at ten seconds each meant standing on that branch costs twenty
seconds of waiting, so it became `StudioConfig.orphanGraceMs` (same default) — the reconciler already took
such a parameter and the host had never passed one through. Three more rules were driven in the same round:
a delivery's samples are the PROFILE's own (the preview ceiling deliberately does not apply — checkable
only when the two ceilings differ), its camera is chosen by role first and declaration order second, and
`listProjects` lists a project whose current revision cannot be read instead of dropping it. `host/lib/index.js`
fell 40 → 26, with 15 of its lines added by this round's own product edit.

── ROUND 69: the decoder that had never read four of its five colour types ──
`contracts/lib/png.js` was the third-darkest file here and nobody had targeted it by name. Its own header
promises "8-bit PNG in the five colour types Blender emits", while the product writes ONE of them (RGBA) and
reads only its own renders — so the arithmetic for greyscale, palette, grey+alpha and 16-bit samples was a
claim nothing ran, one `sample()` index away from being wrong with nothing to notice. The round drove all of
them with PNGs the test builds itself (a writer that follows the FILE's channel order, not the codec's), and
pinned the refusals by message: a colour type PNG does not define, a bit depth the codec does not do, a filter
that does not exist, image data that stops before the last scanline, a palette shorter than three bytes per
entry, plus the encoder's two. `png.js` fell **27 → 2**, and the two lines left are the SHADOWED `default:` in
`readPixel` — the only unexecuted code in the file is code that is proved unreachable, which the test says in
words instead of pretending to cover it.

The round's best find was a SURVIVING mutation: deleting the palette's bounds guard changed no reading at all,
because `palette[i]` past the end is `undefined`, a `Uint8Array` coerces that to 0, and 0 is exactly what the
guard returns. The only input that separates the two is a MALFORMED palette whose length is not a multiple of
three — with one, the unchecked read answers `40,0,0,255` (the palette's fourth byte used as red, half a
colour) where the guard answers black. That case is now in the file, and the mutation is red. This is D141's
third category: not a hole in the assertions and not an unreachable branch, but a guard whose failure mode
has the same answer as the bug on every well-formed input.

── ROUND 70: the ends of a render, and a diagnostics document of the wrong shape ──
`provider-local` (38 dark lines) got the slice around how a render ENDS: a spawn that throws
(`SPAWN_FAILED`, naming the executable that was never started), a child that dies with no usable result
document AND no captured output at all (three bad facts at once, reported as DATA — `envelope: null`, empty
streams, the rejection kept — because the caller has to fold all three into one job record), the capability
cache and what `dispose()` does to it (measured by counting spawns: one probe, a cached read, then a probe
again), and a diagnostics document whose field TYPES are wrong, where the rule is "answer an empty list"
rather than letting the word `'Apple M1 Max'` decide `gpuAvailable`. One check needed no seam: the PATH
lookup behind the settings card's suggestion runs each candidate (`--version`) before offering it, because a
wrong suggestion costs more than no suggestion.

TWO MUTATIONS WERE AIMED AT THE WRONG CALL SITE and came back green: `BlenderErrorCode.SPAWN_FAILED` and
`spawnFailure = cause` each appear TWICE in this file (`runBootstrap` and `startFrameSequence` /
`awaitFrameSequence`), and the first site is not what the new assertions watch. With the surrounding lines
included as context both went red. Round 55 recorded "an anchor that appears twice is a finding"; the
addition here is that a mutation must be aimed with CONTEXT, or "all red" quietly contains "red for the
wrong reason". `provider-local` fell 38 → 20.

── ROUND 71: a second opinion, and what the product keeps of it ──
`visualReview`'s reviewer port is injectable by design ("tests inject a stub", says its own doc), and what
it does with an answer had never been asserted. Driven: the port receives the review, the views, the
iteration and the sheet's real PNG BYTES (a vision reviewer that cannot see the image is not one); findings
are validated against the views and objects THIS render produced, so one naming a view that was never
rendered is rejected WITH its reason instead of being counted ("a reviewer's word is only worth as much as
the evidence under it"); and what the reviewer said is kept as its own record — model, provider, note, raw,
the operations COUNTED — separate from what was accepted. The default is "do not spend a model call", and a
promise about NOT doing something is checked the only way it can be: with a port that would fail loudly if
it were consulted.

The same round drove a defensive half of a port CONTRACT: `resolveBlenderExecutable` documents
`{resolved: string|null, requested, error: BlenderError|null}` and never promises the two are linked, so
both entry points carry `error ?? new BlenderError(NOT_FOUND, ...)`. The real method cannot produce "no path
and no error" (its catch always builds one), which is why it was driven by replacing the port itself — a
resolver answering `{resolved: null, error: null}` makes both entry points refuse by name instead of
dereferencing nothing later. Two mutations aimed at the two `??` fallbacks are red, so the sentences are
load-bearing rather than decorative. `host/lib/index.js` 26 → 20, `provider-local/lib/index.js` 20 → 14.

── ROUND 72: the sentences an operator reads, and the ones about TIME ──
`contracts/lib/ui-api.js` had 13 dark lines and almost all of them were one thing: text a PERSON reads.
`describeJobForHuman` had only ever been called for a job with no estimate, so the branch that says how long
is left — and `formatDuration` underneath it — had never produced a single character. A wrong 预计还剩 is a
promise about the next hour of somebody's day, so the readings are pinned at the boundaries (`—` for a
missing or negative duration rather than `0 秒` or `NaN 秒`; `59 秒` / `1 分 0 秒` / `59 分 59 秒` /
`1 小时 0 分` / `1 小时 30 分`), an unknown status is still DESCRIBED rather than dropped, a track with no
keyframes reports no frame range instead of one built from nothing, a failed headless-render self-test says
WHY (and does not print `undefined` when there is no reason), and a MALFORMED percent-escape is kept
verbatim so the path guard answers 404 instead of the router throwing before a response exists — round 39's
"decode before guarding" had a second half, and this is it. The file went from 13 dark lines to **none**,
and the number of files with any dark lines fell 21 → 20 for the first time in several rounds.

── ROUND 73: the SECOND case of one patch ──
Four of `scene-patch.js`'s dark branches needed nothing but a second input: the fixture declares no world, no
assets, and had only ever removed the LAST item of a collection. With a second case each, the product says
which of two facts happened: the first `world.set` "set the scene world (color …, strength …)" and the
second says **`replaced the scene world`**; removing ONE of two assets leaves the collection holding the
other rather than dropping the key ("this part is empty" and "this project has no assets" are different
things); an `entity.add` referencing an asset the scene does not declare is `PATCH_REFERENCE_MISSING` with
both ids in the sentence; and removing a camera that was never added is `PATCH_TARGET_MISSING` naming the
camera it looked for. Both refusals also assert the spec is byte-identical: a patch either happens or
changes nothing.

The round's best assertion is two words long in the output: `project.frameRange.set` answers
`specChanged: true, sceneChanged: false`. That is D20 in one line — a frame-range change is VISIBLE and must
not trigger a re-render — and the two mutations that pin either half (never report a spec change; call every
patch a scene change) are red, the second one taking an older check down with it. `scene-patch.js` fell
13 → 6, and all six remaining lines are the branch round 57 already named as SHADOWED by the schema: the
file is finished, in the sense that every line is either executed or proved unreachable.

── ROUND 74: what the model is told about a render ──
`tool-plane-output.test.mjs` had always driven the M1 tools, and its stub had no notion of a render job at
all — which is why the M3 tools' failure and empty-state text had never been executed in this layer: the
composition suite that drives them needs a real Blender host and therefore only ever reaches success. A
render-only host stub (`hostApiVersion`, `listJobs`, `getJob`, `resumeRenderJob`, `startFinalRender`,
`exportProject`, `cancelJob`) made six states reachable, and all six sentences are now asserted: an empty job
list says which tool starts one; an unfinished job is named WITH the tool that continues it; a job list whose
reconciliation could not READ says so on the same answer (the field round 67 gave a real producer to); a
resume that must re-render an incomplete frame says which frame and why; an over-threshold render asks the
OPERATOR — the prompt's own words are asserted, including the measured 19.6-41.4 s per frame — and a decline
leaves the model with the number it crossed and "nothing was started"; an export that encoded but did not
verify says NOT published and shows the disagreeing fields; and a cancel that throws an unclassified error
becomes a coded failure, not a stack.

One stub mistake is worth recording: the first version lacked `hostApiVersion`, and four of the six cases
answered "host services are present but too OLD" — the staleness handshake runs before everything else, and
"my stub is incomplete" reads exactly like "the host is old", so the fix is to complete the stub rather than
loosen the assertion. The shared tool-plane harness gained an optional `services` map for the same round's
approval case, because "was the operator asked" and "what is said when nobody can be asked" are two
questions, and answering the first should not require a third copy of how to stand up the tool plane.
── ROUND 103: still no probe — a security claim held against the code ──
No product code changed, so the previous column still describes this tree (the parallel contributor's files are
byte-identical, md5). What the round did was read `SECURITY.md` as a list of checkable claims: four of its
sentences are true and one was wrong — "the child's environment is a five-variable whitelist" while the provider
whitelists SIX names (and `deepblend/docs/security.md` had listed all six correctly the whole time). A count
inside a security document is exactly the kind of sentence a reader trusts and nobody re-measures.

`SECURITY.md` now names where the list lives instead of counting it ("a count is the part of a sentence that
rots"), and the existing whitelist check gained two invariants it did not have: the two spawn paths must
whitelist the SAME names (the old check only required each key to be on the allowed list — a subset, which is the
safe direction, but a path missing `DEEPBLEND_JOB_ID` would have gone unremarked), and the documented list must
equal the code's set while the top-level file may not state a count at all. Four mutations, all red.

── ROUND 102: still no probe — a check on the docs' own examples ──
No product code changed, so no new reading: the previous column still describes this tree (the parallel
contributor's files are byte-identical, md5), and the 53 dark lines are the same 32 named plus their 21.

What the round added is a check rather than a measurement: every ```json block in the documentation must be
JSON, and a block shaped like a ScenePatch must be a patch the product ACCEPTS. Two of the ten blocks are
intentional elisions (a job record with `...`, an NDJSON stream) and are skipped by name, with a guard that the
allowance is still exercised. SPEC §8.3's patch example validates as written — which is the property worth
pinning rather than a defect worth fixing, and the same rule the tool advice follows: something a reader is
told to write has to be something the product takes.

Two pits were hit while writing it. The scope was too narrow (only the four manuals plus README and SPEC), so a
mutation that removed an elision marker from a BRIEF's example stayed green — a check that skips a directory is
one whose scope cannot be seen from its name. And two of the mutations turned out not to be mutations at all:
one removed a single `...` from a block that has several, and the other removed them all, which made the block
VALID JSON.

── ROUND 120: no probe — a dark line explained by a property instead of an observation ──
No product code changed. `json-schema.js` ends its type switch with `default: return false`, and that line is dark
in every reading — the claim being that no bundled schema asks for an unknown type. That claim is worth asserting
rather than assuming, because the failure it hides is silent and total: a schema written `"type": "int"` makes the
validator reject every value at that position, and the symptom is a feature that mysteriously refuses its own
valid input.

The check walks the schemas the way the validator does (every nested object and array) and requires the types to
be ones it implements, with a guard so a walk over zero types cannot pass. A second, behavioural assertion pins
WHY the default branch is dead: a schema declaring `"type": "int"` must be refused when a value is validated
against it, which is a guard rather than luck.

Three misreadings happened first, and all three are the same shape as earlier rounds: the first check called a
type ARRAY unsupported and reported three real properties of `job-result.schema.json` as broken (the truth was in
the caller — `Array.isArray(node.type) ? node.type : [node.type]` — because I read the switch and not who calls
it); the probe called `compileSchema` alone, which walks keywords, while type names are checked when a value is
validated; and the first mutation was aimed at the authoritative schema copy while this check walks the packaged
mirror (the mirror-drift check lives in another suite).

── ROUND 119: no probe — the fourth `--check`, and the half of it this machine cannot reach ──
No product code changed. `install-blender.mjs` keeps its managed install inside the repository
(`MANAGED_TOOLS_DIRECTORY` is `.tools`, `TOOLS = join(ROOT, '.tools')`) and offers no override, so driving its
drift branch would mean moving a real Blender or editing the pinned release record — the machine rather than a
fixture. What is reachable is asserted: where an install exists it must verify clean, and where none exists it
must not report success (its documented 0 means "installed and matching", and the contract names no code for
"absent", so the case asserts only what holds either way).

The probe that decides which branch to take was wrong first: it hardcoded `deepblend/tools/Blender.app`, found
nothing, and walked into the "absent" branch on a machine that HAS Blender — and the product's own output is what
exposed it ("the pinned Blender 5.2.1 is installed"). It derives the path from the same constants the installer
uses now.

The coverage is stated with its measurement: the mutation that makes the present branch exit 1 goes red here,
